---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const faqJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Why does chilli powder lose colour over time?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Chilli powder lightens gradually at room temperature as its red carotenoid pigments degrade, and the change is faster after high-temperature sterilisation. Cool, dry, dark, sealed storage slows this."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does colour change mean the chilli has spoiled?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Not necessarily. Gradual lightening in chilli powder and darkening in sweet paprika are normal pigment behaviour. Check moisture and odour to tell normal colour shift from actual spoilage."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do I keep chilli powder colour stable?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Store cool, dry, dark and sealed — keep moisture under 13%, avoid light and oxygen, and avoid high-temperature sterilisation unless required. If sterilisation is required, specify a higher starting ASTA to compensate for the fade."
      }
    }
  ]
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Chilli Powder Colour Change & Storage | Tenda Peppers"
  description="Why chilli powder fades, why sweet paprika darkens, and a storage checklist (cool, dry, dark, sealed) to keep colour stable. Tenda Peppers Knowledge Hub."
  ogType="article"
  author="Tenda Peppers Technical Team"
  datePublished="2026-09-02"
  dateModified="2026-09-02"
  jsonLd={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <div class="container section">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" style="font-size:0.9rem;color:#6b5a54;margin-bottom:16px;">
      <a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/blog/">Guides</a> &rsaquo; Colour Change &amp; Storage
    </nav>
    <h1>Chilli Powder Colour Change &amp; Storage</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Colour is not permanent. Chilli powder fades over time — faster after sterilisation — and
      sweet paprika can darken. Both are normal pigment chemistry, and four storage conditions keep
      colour stable longest.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Chilli colour is not permanent — powder lightens over time, faster after high-temperature sterilisation, and sweet paprika can darken. Both are normal carotenoid chemistry, not a defect. The four levers that slow it: cool, dark, dry, and low-oxygen storage.
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Why chilli powder changes colour</h2>
    <p>
      Chilli and paprika get their red colour from carotenoid pigments, which are sensitive to light,
      heat and oxygen. Two changes matter in practice: chilli powder <strong>lightens</strong> as the
      red carotenoids oxidise, and sweet paprika can <strong>darken</strong> when its higher sugar
      content browns under heat. Neither means the product is spoiled — but the colour it ships with
      is not the colour it will show after months on a shelf.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Product</th><th>Change</th><th>Trigger</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Chilli powder</strong></td><td>Lightens (fades)</td><td>Time; accelerates after high-temperature sterilisation</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Sweet paprika</strong></td><td>Darkens</td><td>High-temperature sterilisation, then time</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The storage checklist</h2>
    <p>Four conditions, in order of importance:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Cool</strong> — heat accelerates colour loss</li>
      <li><strong>Dry</strong> — keep moisture under 13%</li>
      <li><strong>Dark</strong> — light breaks down carotenoids</li>
      <li><strong>Sealed</strong> — limit oxygen, which drives oxidation</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">When sterilisation is required</h2>
    <p>
      Some buyers require high-temperature sterilisation for microbial control. If so, expect the
      colour to shift — fading for chilli, darkening for sweet paprika — and build it into your
      specification by setting a higher starting ASTA. See the full
      <a href="https://docs.drychillis.com/colour-storage/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">colour &amp; storage reference</a> in the Knowledge Hub.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Why does chilli powder lose colour over time?</h3>
      <p>Chilli powder lightens gradually at room temperature as its red carotenoid pigments degrade, and the change is faster after high-temperature sterilisation. Cool, dry, dark, sealed storage slows this.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Does colour change mean the chilli has spoiled?</h3>
      <p>Not necessarily. Gradual lightening in chilli powder and darkening in sweet paprika are normal pigment behaviour. Check moisture and odour to tell normal colour shift from actual spoilage.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>How do I keep chilli powder colour stable?</h3>
      <p>Store cool, dry, dark and sealed — keep moisture under 13%, avoid light and oxygen, and avoid high-temperature sterilisation unless required. If sterilisation is required, specify a higher starting ASTA to compensate for the fade.</p>
    </div>

    <div style="margin-top:32px;">
      <a class="cta" href="/products/dried-chillies/">Browse Dried Chillies</a>
      <a class="cta cta-outline" href="/contact/" style="margin-left:8px;">Request a Quote</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
